WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Back in June, at AI DevCon in London, one idea came up in almost every room

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.960
that the hard part of working with AI is no longer the prompt.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.800
And it isn't the model either.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.560
It's the context you put around it.

5
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.280
Let's first hear from Lamis

6
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.440
Mukta at Anthropic, who walked us through how context engineering has changed over

7
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.360
the past year, and why she thinks it's the thing that multiplies everything else.

8
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.880
By way of introduction, my name is Lamis.

9
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.480
I'm a member of technical staff at Anthropic.

10
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.960
I work on our applied AI team, and this is a team

11
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.240
which sits between research, product and go-to-market.

12
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.960
So we do a mixture of working on internal projects as well as directly

13
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.280
with customers who are building agents at the frontier.

14
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.120
Me specifically, I work with startups and founders,

15
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.640
many of whom I'm sure are in this room today,

16
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.040
and I think I have the best seat in the house

17
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.200
because these are the users that are constantly pushing us right up

18
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.080
against the boundary of what is possible with models and products today.

19
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.400
And as such, we just get to, like, ride the exponential together.

20
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.040
One thing that constantly comes up, as I'm sure you're all aware,

21
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.280
is what it really takes to take the raw model intelligence

22
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.880
that we have today and translate that into durable, scalable, useful products.

23
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.080
And one of the main levers that we have in order to do

24
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.320
this is context engineering, which will be the focus of my talk today.

25
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.200
So on this journey, I want to

26
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.920
quickly do a recap of where context engineering has gone in the past year.

27
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.800
It's a space that's completely blown up, and through that,

28
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.760
we'll kind of distill the primitives that have proven

29
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.080
to be really useful, some stuff that has been a little bit less useful.

30
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.400
Secondly, we'll talk about

31
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.640
what the state of the art is for memory management today.

32
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.000
And thirdly, and in particular with that, we'll talk about not just what

33
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.360
nice theoretical principles are,

34
00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.680
but what it takes to actually build these systems in production.

35
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.520
And then finally, we'll talk about where this will go on the path to continual

36
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.560
learning, and in particular, touching on a paradigm called dreaming.

37
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.680
So we said this before.

38
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.680
And models — we release new models all the time.

39
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.640
They are more and more intelligent.

40
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.920
But when it comes to actually deploying these models in your agents,

41
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.920
in your environments, in your organization,

42
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.200
the intelligence alone is not going to compound because they need this context

43
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.200
that helps them perform the specific tasks that you need them to.

44
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.320
In particular, a lot of this context is often

45
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.800
kind of orthogonal to the model intelligence, right?

46
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.200
Like the newest model we've just released, one isn't going to

47
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.360
isn't going to, out of the box, know exactly what it takes to succeed

48
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.400
in your organization and with the tasks that you want them to.

49
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.240
And so it's a really great investment to work on the context engineering part,

50
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.960
because this over time has the effect of multiplying

51
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.960
the intelligence even as models get smarter.

52
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.520
We also heard from Ryan Lopopolo at OpenAI.

53
00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.760
He argues that most of the old limits on software engineering are gone,

54
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.240
and what's left is human time, attention, and the context window.

55
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.560
He can only really hold three sessions in his head at once. So,

56
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.880
having just told

57
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.440
everybody here that the core constraints on software

58
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.640
engineering no longer apply, what are those core constraints, right?

59
00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.440
We have kind of a new set of problems to contend with, using agents

60
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.000
in order to produce our software.

61
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.160
And to me, these three things are the foundational limits that remain

62
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.880
in a world where we are, as a team of humans and agents, producing software.

63
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.720
Human time is the fundamentally scarce resource that we have.

64
00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:54.080
You know, I know I max out probably at three concurrent sessions on my laptop.

65
00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.360
If I want to be more parallel and have higher throughput,

66
00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.440
I must find ways to remove my own synchronous attention from the process.

67
00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:04.920
Human and model attention

68
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.640
are these foundational limits, right?

69
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.640
In the architecture of these LLMs, attention must sum to one.

70
00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.600
Thrashing the agents by having them do more and more work

71
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.880
with conflicting and overbearing requirements in the course of a task

72
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.200
is something that is always going to degrade performance less

73
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.040
and less over time, but it is one of those core limits of the models.

74
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.320
So we need to retool the way we work in order to be more parallel.

75
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.920
Fork off a bunch more tasks, be willing to accept smaller or larger

76
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.440
or many more PRs in order to let the agents explore what it means to

77
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.280
do the job that we need them to do.

78
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.280
And finally, you all probably deeply live this model context window.

79
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.560
Things that get bigger over time.

80
00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.560
Still a scarce resource, something we need to protect.

81
00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.320
I will say, in my own experience with the GPT series of models,

82
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.840
auto-compaction is fantastic.

83
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.120
I never think about a context window anymore.

84
00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:03.200
I can let a task go for six, 12, 36 hours and still get good results,

85
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.920
but the context window being obliterated and rebuilt

86
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.440
over the course of these auto-compactions is something you must contend with.

87
00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.040
And there are ways that we structure the context

88
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:14.960
we give the model, or

89
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.800
continually resurface context to the model to deal with this constraint,

90
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.800
that context windows are continually being emptied and filled.

91
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.560
Birgitta Böckeler is a Distinguished Engineer at Thoughtworks.

92
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.520
She splits the work of building a harness into two halves: guides

93
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.520
that point the agent forward, and sensors that tell it how it's doing.

94
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.160
So we're starting with these guides.

95
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.800
But then we also want to give it feedback.

96
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:38.240
Right.

97
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.200
So, ideally, so that we can trigger immediately

98
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:43.520
a self-correction loop

99
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.520
before we even look at the code so that we don't have to, like,

100
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.800
have all those low-hanging fruit still in there.

101
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.800
So the most common way that people do

102
00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.880
that right now is with code review agents, right?

103
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.040
But there's also all of these other tools that we have in our toolbox

104
00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.080
from before AI, like static code analysis.

105
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.960
And then, of course, an agent usually has access to the log

106
00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.440
so it can start the application, see what logs come out of it.

107
00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.920
Many people give an agent access to the browser so it can look at, like,

108
00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.480
something when it has changed a web component or something like that.

109
00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.240
And there's actually a difference between these.

110
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.560
Sort of, like, a review agent is an LLM judging the work of another LLM.

111
00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:25.800
Right.

112
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.080
So it's kind of inferential.

113
00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.440
It's running on the GPU, but we have a bunch of tools as well

114
00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.400
that are computational, as I decided to call them here.

115
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.840
So kind of things that run on the CPU, right, like the static analysis

116
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.000
is the best example, I think, to think about this.

117
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:42.520
Yeah.

118
00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.680
And we have the same distinction on the feed-forward, on the guide side.

119
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.920
So we can also think about computational guides on that side.

120
00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.400
And the best example for me there is codemods. Ian from Meta

121
00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.560
also just mentioned those, which is, for example, tools like OpenRewrite

122
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:07.560
that are really good at doing version upgrades and migrations of frameworks.

123
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.560
I don't know if you remember,

124
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.360
like, quite a while ago, Amazon had a really big headline about

125
00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.880
saving 400 or 500 developer years or something for Java upgrades.

126
00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.080
That was, under the hood, actually mostly codemods being made available to AI.

127
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.240
So that combination is really powerful, right?

128
00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:27.720
So all of these things, or maybe providing a different type of code search

129
00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.760
that is more effective for your really large code base.

130
00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.520
All of those are ways, again, to increase the probability that AI does

131
00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.560
what you want in the first go.

132
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.320
And to close, Guy Podjarny, CEO and co-founder of Tessl.

133
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.160
He makes the case that all of this adds up to a whole new life

134
00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.200
cycle, sitting alongside the one we already know.

135
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:52.720
Humans move into the context development lifecycle and the agents

136
00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.160
get the software one.

137
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:56.880
And at the end of the day, we can build really quality software.

138
00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.040
But there's only so far you can get in the lab.

139
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.880
And so you can build your software, you can test it, you can secure it,

140
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.680
you can optimize it. And then at some point you need to step out.

141
00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.480
And that comes into observability for agents.

142
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.480
Runtime observability is monitoring the coding agents.

143
00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:12.480
And when you monitor

144
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.800
those, you can use those to improve the quality of what you have.

145
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.800
So you can extract real-world eval scenarios and problems

146
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.840
and use those to update and optimize your skills.

147
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.560
And you can mine for gaps.

148
00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:27.080
So you can look at agents' success as a whole and

149
00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:28.800
extract out of

150
00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.800
that opportunities for new skills or things that you should remove.

151
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.120
And in general, as you think about this whole process, combining

152
00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.400
all these tools together, you increasingly should see a development lifecycle.

153
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.480
But it should be a context development lifecycle, not a skill,

154
00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.360
not a software development lifecycle.

155
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.600
We should, as humans, live in the context development lifecycle and leave the SDLC

156
00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:54.880
to the agents, or be able to enable the agents to do the SDLC,

157
00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:58.000
in which we generate context, evaluate and test it,

158
00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.560
optimize it as we need to, distribute it via good package management.

159
00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.800
Securely consume them, observe what has happened, and do that again and again.

160
00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.840
So in summary,

161
00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:13.640
we have a new software stack for agent development.

162
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.200
It builds on the models that are like our operating systems.

163
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.560
We need to make sure what we build is compatible with them.

164
00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.000
We have tools as utilities.

165
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.640
We have context as the new code.

166
00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:23.640
We have harnesses that are like frameworks.

167
00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:24.960
Not every developer will

168
00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.040
build their own harness, but probably more and more organizations would

169
00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.080
substantially customize harnesses if not build their own, with those

170
00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.520
composing into factory lines that feel very much like pipelines.

171
00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.560
You have repeatable type of input coming in, successful

172
00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.240
output coming out, and into whole dev processes in factories.

173
00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.640
And within those, skills are the new code,

174
00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.960
and we should treat them that way and give them the right tools for it.

175
00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.120
Thanks for watching.

176
00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.760
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

177
00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.720
Visit AI DevCon to learn more and book your ticket.
